    title: "The 90% You'll Lose by Tuesday",
    generated: true,
    challengeRating: 5,
    prompt: "You're a sales-ops analyst with a 15-minute slot at Thursday's leadership meeting. Your goal is simple: get the VPs to approve a $40k spend on a new lead-scoring tool. You have a mountain of supporting evidence — pilot conversion lifts, a vendor comparison grid, a payback-period model, three customer quotes, and competitor benchmarks. You know that by the time these execs actually decide (probably in their Tuesday budget review, four days later), most of what you say will have evaporated from memory. With your prep time, what's the highest-leverage move to make sure the room walks out able to act on the one thing you came for?",
    correct: "Pick the single sentence you want them repeating on Tuesday — \"This tool pays for itself in four months\" — and engineer the talk so that line lands early, gets restated at each turn, and closes the talk; let the evidence exist to back it up, not to compete with it.",
    wrong: [
      ["Build out every supporting slide in full — the comparison grid, the payback model, all three customer quotes — so the case is airtight and no VP can poke a hole in it.", "It feels responsible: more proof seems like more persuasion, and you dread the question you didn't cover. But audiences retain only about 10% of a talk a few days out, and a wall of equally-weighted evidence gives them nothing to retain — they leave with an impression of 'lots of data' and no sentence to repeat in the room where the decision actually happens.", "Ask which ONE line you want quoted in the Tuesday budget review, then demote everything else to backup that defends that line on demand."],
      ["Rehearse a polished, word-for-word script for all 15 minutes so your delivery is smooth and confident and nothing comes out as rambling.", "Smoothness feels like the thing that wins rooms, so memorizing every word seems like the safe investment. It's a real technique, but it's overkill aimed at the wrong target: a flawless recital of fifteen forgettable minutes still leaves the room with no single takeaway, and the effort buys polish instead of memorability — and a memorized script shatters the moment a VP interrupts with a question.", "Spend that rehearsal energy on making one sentence land hard and repeatably, not on smoothing fourteen sentences nobody will keep."],
      ["Open by flagging that the data is still a bit preliminary and you're not a finance expert, so they'll know to weigh your numbers carefully.", "It feels like honesty and humility — heading off skepticism before it's raised. But pre-emptive apology in the opening spends your most-remembered moment (primacy) installing doubt about the exact thing you need them to act on; you've made your one shot at a clear ask into a disclaimer.", "Use the opening to plant the takeaway, not to discount it — caveat the methodology in the backup if someone digs, not in the line they'll carry out the door."],
    ],
    mentorHint: "Don't ask \"how much can I cover?\" — ask \"what one sentence do I want them repeating in the room where the decision actually happens?\"",
    lesson: "The hidden variable in this scenario isn't the meeting on Thursday — it's the decision on Tuesday, four days later, in a room you won't be in. That gap is where most workplace talks quietly fail. Research on retention is brutal and consistent: people lose roughly 90% of what they hear within about 48 hours, and a week out you're down to around 10%. So the real design question is never \"how much can I tell them?\" It's \"what one sentence do I want surviving into the room where the choice gets made?\" Once you frame it that way, piling on slides stops looking thorough and starts looking like dilution — every additional equally-weighted point lowers the odds that any single point is the one that sticks.\n\nThis is why the elegant move beats the effortful ones. Choosing one takeaway (\"pays for itself in four months\"), planting it early (primacy), restating it at each transition, and landing on it at the close (recency) is cheap to prepare and exploits exactly how memory actually works. The mountain of evidence isn't wasted — it just changes job. It moves from the foreground (things you narrate) to the background (things you can reach for when a VP pushes back). A talk with one load-bearing sentence and a deep bench of backup is far more persuasive than a talk where the grid, the model, and the quotes all shout at equal volume.\n\nNotice the trap shared by the tempting answers: each one invests real effort in the wrong place. More slides, a memorized script, a humble disclaimer — all feel diligent, and one of them (the full script) genuinely is a legitimate technique. But none of them increases the probability that the decision-makers can repeat your ask on Tuesday, which is the only outcome the talk exists to produce. The shortcut isn't laziness; it's putting your limited prep behind the one move with leverage.",
    solution: "Foundational idea: a workplace talk is a service to the listener, and the service here is making it effortless for them to carry your ask into a decision they make later, without you. That reframes \"preparation\" entirely — you're not building the most complete case, you're building the most transferable one.\n\nThree things to internalize:\n1. Memory decays fast and unevenly. Audiences keep a small fraction of what you say within a couple of days, and they keep the beginning and the end better than the middle (primacy and recency). Design to those facts: put the takeaway where memory is strongest and repeat it so decay has less to erase. The rule of three is the same instinct at work — three is roughly what short-term memory holds, which is why \"more points\" past a few actively hurts recall.\n2. Evidence has two jobs, and they're different. Foreground evidence is what you narrate and what competes for the audience's limited attention; background evidence is what you keep ready to defend your one claim when challenged. Most over-stuffed talks fail because they promote all their backup to the foreground, leaving the audience with volume instead of a verdict.\n3. The opening is your single most-remembered moment. Spend it planting the one thing, not apologizing, not throat-clearing, not hedging. (And resist the Mehrabian-myth temptation that delivery polish — the supposed \"93% nonverbal\" — outweighs having a clear message; that statistic was about communicating feelings in a lab, not about whether a budget gets approved.)\n\nPractical drill: before any talk, write the literal sentence you want a listener repeating to someone else 48 hours later. If you can't write it, the audience can't carry it. Then build the talk backward from that sentence — open with it, return to it at each transition, close on it — and relegate everything else to \"available if asked.\"",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "You've got fifteen minutes and a pile of evidence to win a $40k budget approval, but the people in the room won't actually decide until their meeting four days from now. Where should you put the bulk of your effort so that, when they sit down to decide without you, they can still act on what you asked for?" },
